Semantic search requires a vector on the entry and a means of embedding the search phrase. These are two
separate pieces of configuration in two different locations, and they must agree with one another.

# **1. Attach the vector preprocessor to the listener**

In **/opt/gravwell/etc/gravwell\_llm\_ingester.conf**:

<pre>
[Listener "openai"]
	Bind = ":4180"
	Upstream-URL = "https://api.openai.com"
	Protocol = "openai-chat"
	Tag-Name = llm
	Log-Tool-Calls = true
	Log-Usage = true
	Preprocessor = embed

[Preprocessor "embed"]
	Type = vector
	Model = text-embedding-3-small
	Endpoint = https://api.openai.com/v1/embeddings
	Token = YOUR\_EMBEDDING\_API\_TOKEN
	Timeout = 15
	Retry-Attempts = 3
	Passthrough-On-Error = true
</pre>

**Model** and **Endpoint** are required. The endpoint must be a full URL including the scheme, and must
be OpenAI-compatible. **Token** is sent as an **Authorization: Bearer** header.

Every entry in a processing block is embedded in a single request, which limits the number of round
trips. Empty and whitespace-only payloads bypass the API entirely. The entry payload is never modified;
the vector is attached as an enumerated value named **embeddings**, holding the JSON encoding of the
array.

**Set Passthrough-On-Error=true unless losing entries is preferable to losing embeddings.** With it
disabled, a failing embeddings endpoint results in lost entries.

Restart the ingester and confirm that vectors are being attached:

```
tag=$LLM_TAG $LLM_USER_PROMPTS model embeddings
| eval has(embeddings)
| stats count as Embedded by model
| table model Embedded
```

**embeddings** is an intrinsic enumerated value like every other field on this tag, so it must be
declared on the **intrinsic** module before anything -- **eval**, or the **semantic** module itself --
can read it. **$LLM\_USER\_PROMPTS** supplies the **intrinsic** module, and **model embeddings** are
appended to it.

# **2. Configure the embedding model on the webserver**

The **semantic** module must embed *the search phrase* at query time, and it does so from the webserver.
Add the following to the **[AI]** section of **/opt/gravwell/etc/gravwell.conf**:

<pre>
[AI]
	Embedding-URL="https://api.openai.com/v1/embeddings"
	Embedding-Model="text-embedding-3-small"
	Embedding-Token="YOUR\_EMBEDDING\_API\_TOKEN"
</pre>

Restart the webserver. Requests to this endpoint use a 10 second timeout.

# **The two models must match**

This is the most common configuration error. The model in the vector preprocessor and the model in
**[AI]** each produce vectors of a particular dimensionality. If they differ, the cosine comparison is
meaningless, and the **semantic** module fails the query on dimension mismatch rather than returning
incorrect results.

Use the same model name in both locations. If the embedding model is changed later, entries embedded
with the previous model are no longer comparable; either reingest them or accept that hunts reach back
only as far as the changeover.

Both may be pointed at a self-hosted endpoint. **Embedding-Model="qwen3-embedding:8b"** against a local
server is a supported configuration, and it keeps prompt text within the local network.

# **Resource costs**

**Throughput.** Embedding occurs synchronously during ingest, so the data flow is bounded by the
response time of the embeddings endpoint. Do not attach the vector preprocessor to a high-volume stream
without measuring first.

**Storage.** A 1024-dimensional vector serialised as JSON is roughly 10-20KB *per entry*, which
generally exceeds the size of the prompt it describes. Size the well accordingly.

**Query cost.** The **semantic** module runs on the webserver, so every entry it evaluates has its
vector transferred from the indexers to the webserver first. This is substantially more expensive than
text filtering, and it is the reason every hunt in this kit filters by tag, timeframe, and
**event_type** before the **semantic** module receives an entry.

# **Embedding selectively**

Embedding the full data set is not required. Where the subject of interest is what users are asking for,
attach the vector preprocessor to a listener whose **Log-Mode** is **user**, and operate a second
listener without the preprocessor for all other traffic. This yields searchable prompts at a fraction of
the vector storage.

Continue with **LLM Observability - Hunting Prompts by Meaning**.
